.TH mbpy_grdtiff 1 "3 November 2026" "MB-System 5.0" "MB-System 5.0"
.SH NAME
\fBmbpy_grdtiff\fP \- Create an executable shellscript which will generate
a georeferenced TIFF image of gridded data using GMT modern mode.

.SH VERSION
Version 5.0

.SH SYNOPSIS
\fBmbpy_grdtiff\fP \fB\-I\fP\fIgrid\fP [\fB\-O\fP\fIroot\fP]
[\fB\-X\fP] [\fB\-V\fP]
.br
[\fB\-R\fP\fIwest/east/south/north\fP]
[\fB\-N\fP\fIdx/dy\fP]
.br
[\fB\-G\fP\fIcolor_mode\fP]
[\fB\-W\fP\fIcolor_style/palette\fP[\fI/ncolors\fP] | \fIcptfile\fP]
[\fB\-D\fP] [\fB\-Y\fP] [\fB\-S\fP]
[\fB\-Z\fP\fIzmin/zmax\fP[\fI/mode\fP]]
[\fB\-\-data\-scale\fP \fIfactor\fP]
.br
[\fB\-A\fP\fImagnitude\fP[\fI/azimuth\fP[\fI/elevation\fP]]]
[\fB\-\-magnitude\fP \fIval\fP]
[\fB\-\-azimuth\fP \fIdeg\fP]
[\fB\-\-elevation\fP \fIdeg\fP]
[\fB\-\-shade\-flip\fP]
[\fB\-K\fP\fIintensity_grid\fP]
[\fB\-\-stretch\-shade\fP]
.br
[\fB\-\-gmt\-default\fP \fIparam/value\fP]

.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBmbpy_grdtiff\fP is a Python/PyGMT-era replacement for the Perl macro
\fBmbm_grdtiff\fP. It reads a grid (for instance one made by
\fBmbgrid\fP or \fBmbmosaic\fP) and generates an executable shellscript
of \fBGMT\fP \fBmodern mode\fP commands which, when run, makes a
georeferenced TIFF image of the grid with the \fBMB-System\fP GMT
module \fBmbgrdtiff\fP. The image is a GeoTIFF with the grid's
coordinate system embedded, plus a parallel ".tfw" world file for
software that does not read the GeoTIFF tags. Geographic grids and
grids in projected coordinate systems (e.g. UTM) are both handled. Such
images can be loaded into GIS packages such as QGIS and ArcGIS as
geographically located coverages.

The image may be a color fill of the gridded values, color shaded
relief, a color fill shaded by a separate intensity grid, a color fill
of the slope magnitude, or a color fill shaded by the slope magnitude
(see \fB\-G\fP). The color palettes, color intervals and shading
methods are the same as those of \fBmbpy_grdplot\fP, so a GeoTIFF and a
map of the same grid made with the same options look the same.

The generated script is named \fIroot\fP_tiff.sh and creates
\fIroot\fP.tif and \fIroot\fP.tfw. A generated color palette is
written to \fIroot\fP.cpt by \fBmbpy_grdtiff\fP itself. Because
\fBmbgrdtiff\fP writes its image file directly, the script opens a GMT
modern-mode session without a figure (\fBgmt begin\fP ... \fBgmt end\fP)
only so that its temporary GMT defaults stay inside the session; no
gmt.conf file is created or deleted in the current directory.

Differences from \fBmbm_grdtiff\fP: a file listing several grids is not
accepted by \fB\-I\fP (use one \fBmbpy_grdtiff\fP command per grid);
the \fB\-MGW\fP option is not needed because \fBmbgrdtiff\fP always
writes the world file; the \fB\-MGS\fP option is \fB\-\-data\-scale\fP;
the sealevel palettes 8 and 9 of \fBmbpy_grdplot\fP are available;
discrete palettes and the histogram-equalized color stretch follow
\fBmbm_grdplot\fP and \fBmbpy_grdplot\fP; and the image is not
displayed after it is made.

\fBmbpy_grdtiff\fP requires \fBGMT\fP 6 or later with the
\fBMB-System\fP GMT modules, and a Python 3 interpreter. It uses the
modules \fBmbpy_layout.py\fP, \fBmbpy_color.py\fP, \fBmbpy_misc.py\fP,
\fBmbpy_grdplot_script.py\fP and \fBmbpy_grdtiff_script.py\fP,
installed alongside it.

