.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-I\fP, \fB\-\-input\fP \fIgrid\fP
Input grid file. Required.
.TP
.B \-O\fP, \fB\-\-output\fP \fIroot\fP
Output root name: the script is \fIroot\fP_tiff.sh, the image
\fIroot\fP.tif and the world file \fIroot\fP.tfw. Default: the input
grid's basename without its extension.
.TP
.B \-X\fP, \fB\-\-execute
Run the generated script immediately after writing it.
.TP
.B \-V\fP, \fB\-\-verbose
Print the generated script's path and a summary of the image style,
region, z range and file names.
.TP
.B \-R\fP, \fB\-\-region\fP \fIwest/east/south/north\fP
Bounds of the image, in the grid's own coordinates. When given, the
color stretch spans the z range of the grid inside these bounds.
Default: the grid's own bounds.
.TP
.B \-N\fP, \fB\-\-nudge\fP \fIdx/dy\fP
Shift the location of the image by \fIdx\fP meters east and \fIdy\fP
meters north (\fBmbgrdtiff \-N\fP).
.TP
.B \-G\fP, \fB\-\-color\-mode\fP \fIcolor_mode\fP
Image style:
.br
  \fIcolor_mode\fP = 1: Color fill.
.br
  \fIcolor_mode\fP = 2: Color shaded relief, using an illumination
model built from \fB\-\-magnitude\fP, \fB\-\-azimuth\fP and
\fB\-\-elevation\fP.
.br
  \fIcolor_mode\fP = 3: Color fill shaded by the intensity grid given
with \fB\-K\fP.
.br
  \fIcolor_mode\fP = 4: Color fill of the slope magnitude.
.br
  \fIcolor_mode\fP = 5: Color fill shaded by the slope magnitude.
.br
Default: 1, or 3 when \fB\-K\fP is given.
.TP
.B \-W\fP, \fB\-\-color\-control\fP \fIcolor_style/palette\fP[\fI/ncolors\fP] | \fIcptfile\fP
Color palette. \fIcolor_style\fP 1 gives a continuous palette, 2 a
discrete one. \fIpalette\fP is one of:
.br
  1: Haxby colors [default]
.br
  2: high intensity colors
.br
  3: low intensity colors
.br
  4: grayscale
.br
  5: uniform gray
.br
  6: uniform black
.br
  7: uniform white
.br
  8, 9: sealevel palettes, with a color break at zero (see
\fBmbpy_grdplot\fP)
.br
\fIncolors\fP is the number of colors [11]. If the argument names an
existing file, that file is used as the color palette table.
.TP
.B \-D\fP, \fB\-\-color\-flip
Reverse the color palette.
.TP
.B \-Y\fP, \fB\-\-no\-nice\-interval
Stretch the colors linearly between the minimum and maximum values
instead of using rounded color intervals.
.TP
.B \-S\fP, \fB\-\-stretch\-color
Use histogram equalization of the grid (\fBgmt grdhisteq\fP) to set
the color boundaries. With palette 8 or 9 the values below and above
zero are equalized separately.
.TP
.B \-Z\fP, \fB\-\-zbounds\fP \fIzmin/zmax\fP[\fI/mode\fP]
Use \fIzmin\fP and \fIzmax\fP instead of the grid's own z range for the
color stretch. If \fImode\fP is 1, the first and last colors are
extended to the grid's own minimum and maximum, so that every value is
colored.
.TP
.B \-\-data\-scale\fP \fIfactor\fP
Multiply the grid values by \fIfactor\fP before coloring and shading,
for instance \-1 to plot depths as topography (\fBmbm_grdtiff
\-MGS\fP).
.TP
.B \-A\fP, \fB\-\-shade\fP \fImagnitude\fP[\fI/azimuth\fP[\fI/elevation\fP]]
Shading parameters in one argument, as in \fBmbm_grdtiff \-A\fP. The
separate options below take precedence.
.TP
.B \-\-magnitude\fP \fIval\fP
Shading magnitude. Defaults: 1.0 for modes 2, 4 and 5; \-0.4 for
mode 3.
.TP
.B \-\-azimuth\fP \fIdeg\fP
Illumination azimuth for mode 2, degrees clockwise from north [0].
.TP
.B \-\-elevation\fP \fIdeg\fP
Illumination elevation for mode 2, degrees above the horizon [30].
.TP
.B \-\-shade\-flip
Reverse the sense of the shading (negates the magnitude).
.TP
.B \-K\fP, \fB\-\-intensity\-file\fP \fIintensity_grid\fP
Intensity grid used to shade the image in mode 3; it must cover the
same area as the input grid.
.TP
.B \-\-stretch\-shade
Histogram-equalize the intensity grid before shading (mode 3).
.TP
.B \-\-gmt\-default\fP \fIparam/value\fP
Override a GMT default parameter in the generated script; repeatable.

.SH EXAMPLES
A color shaded relief GeoTIFF of a bathymetry grid, illuminated from
the northeast, made at once:

.br
 mbpy_grdtiff \-I ZTopo.grd \-G2 \-\-azimuth 45 \-X \-V

A grayscale amplitude mosaic shaded by a separate intensity grid:

.br
 mbpy_grdtiff \-I ZAmp.grd \-O ZAmp_shaded \-K ZTopoInt.grd \\
.br
     \-W 1/4 \-\-stretch\-shade

A topography image of a depth grid (positive down), with the colors
clipped to 1000 to 4000 m depth but every value still colored:

.br
 mbpy_grdtiff \-I depths.grd \-\-data\-scale \-1 \-Z \-4000/\-1000/1

